---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc842.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Single Lessee Model" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Lease Classification — Finance vs Operating" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Right-of-Use Asset Measurement" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Lease Liability Measurement" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Lessor Accounting — Three-Model Retention" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Sale-Leaseback Transactions" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Short-Term Leases and Low-Value Assets" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example — Lessee ROU Asset and Lease Liability" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "ASC 842 vs IFRS 16 — Comprehensive Comparison" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Bringing virtually all leases onto the balance sheet</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 842 fundamentally transformed US GAAP lease accounting by requiring lessees to recognise RIGHT-OF-USE (ROU) assets and corresponding lease liabilities for virtually all leases with terms greater than 12 months. This eliminates the off-balance-sheet accounting that was the defining feature — and primary criticism — of ASC 840. The standard is control-based rather than form-based: a lessee recognises an ROU asset when it controls the use of an identified asset for a period of time in exchange for consideration.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">A US-specific compromise on lessor accounting</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">While ASC 842 fully converged lessee accounting with IFRS 16's single model, the FASB deliberately retained a three-model lessor classification (sales-type, direct financing, and operating leases) to preserve alignment with US GAAP's historical lessor framework and to avoid a more radical departure from existing practice. This compromise means that the lessor sides of ASC 842 and IFRS 16 remain structurally divergent — a rare instance where US GAAP retained more complexity on one side of a transaction than IFRS.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">All leases of identified assets with control</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 842 applies to all leases of identified assets in which the lessee obtains the right to control the use of the asset for a period of time in exchange for consideration. The standard covers leases of property, plant, equipment, and intangible assets — though certain leases are scoped out, including leases of intangible assets accounted for under other standards, short-term leases (elections available), leases of low-value assets (elections available), and leases of biological assets within the scope of ASC 905.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Single Lessee Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">One model for all lessees — balance-sheet recognition for virtually all leases</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Under ASC 842, every lessee recognises an ROU asset representing its right to use the underlying asset, and a corresponding lease liability representing its obligation to make lease payments. The single lessee model eliminates the capital/operating classification that existed under ASC 840. For lessees, the only classification is between finance leases and operating leases — both are recognised on the balance sheet, but they have different patterns of expense recognition in the income statement.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Lease Classification — Finance vs Operating</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">Finance lease — analogous to ASC 840's capital lease</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">A finance lease is classified when the lease transfers substantially all the risks and rewards incidental to ownership of the underlying asset. The five finance lease criteria are: (1) transfer of ownership by end of term; (2) purchase option that the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise; (3) lease term for a major part of the asset's remaining economic life; (4) present value of payments for substantially all of the asset's fair value; or (5) the asset is so specialised that it has no alternative use to the lessor. A lease meeting ANY one of these criteria is a finance lease.</div></div>
      <div class="card cw"><div class="ctitle cw">Operating lease — residual classification</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">An operating lease is any lease that does NOT meet the finance lease criteria. Under ASC 842, operating leases still result in a balance-sheet asset and liability — a dramatic change from ASC 840. The income statement effect, however, differs: lessee expense is a single straight-line 'lease expense' rather than the front-loaded pattern of depreciation and interest seen in finance leases. This single-expense model is simpler for users but masks the underlying asset and liability components.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Right-of-Use Asset Measurement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Initially the lease liability, subsequently adjusted</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">The ROU asset is initially measured at the AMOUNT of the lease liability, plus any lease payments made at or before the commencement date, plus any initial direct costs, and minus any lease incentives received. Subsequently, the ROU asset is depreciated over the lease term (or useful life if ownership transfers or a bargain purchase option exists). For finance leases, the ROU asset is reduced by accumulated depreciation and impairment, and the carrying amount equals the lease liability minus any prepaid or accrued lease payments. For operating leases, the ROU asset is simply depreciated on a straight-line basis.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Lease Liability Measurement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Present value of lease payments at the commencement date</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">The lease liability is initially measured at the PRESENT VALUE of the lease payments not yet paid, discounted using the interest rate implicit in the lease (if readily determinable) or the lessee's incremental borrowing rate. Lease payments include: fixed payments; variable payments that depend on an index or rate; amounts expected to be paid under residual value guarantees; and the exercise price of a purchase option if the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise it. The liability is subsequently remeasured only when there is a change in the lease term or reassessment of the lessee's reasonable certainty of exercising a purchase option.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Lessor Accounting — Three-Model Retention</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Sales-type, direct financing, and operating leases</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 842 retains ASC 840's three-model classification for lessors, a deliberate US-specific divergence from IFRS 16's single model. <strong>Sales-type leases</strong> — when the lessor's selling profit is significant — derecognise the underlying asset and recognise a net investment in the lease, with profit recognised at commencement. <strong>Direct financing leases</strong> — when the lessor's cost approximates fair value — also produce a net investment but with profit recognised over the lease term as interest income. <strong>Operating leases</strong> — the residual classification — leave the underlying asset on the lessor's books, with lease revenue recognised on a straight-line basis.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Sale-Leaseback Transactions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Gain recognition depends on lease classification and control</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 842 significantly revised sale-leaseback accounting. If the transaction qualifies as a sale under ASC 606 and the leaseback is classified as a finance lease, the seller-lessee derecognises the underlying asset and recognises the full gain on sale immediately — unlike ASC 840, which generally deferred the gain. If the leaseback is an operating lease, the gain is partially or fully deferred depending on the leaseback's terms. If the transaction DOES NOT qualify as a sale, both parties account for it as a financing arrangement — no sale occurs, and the seller-lessee continues to recognise the asset.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Short-Term Leases and Low-Value Assets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Practical expedients to reduce burden</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 842 provides two practical expedients to reduce the cost of applying the standard. The <strong>short-term lease exemption</strong> allows lessees to elect not to recognise ROU assets and lease liabilities for leases with a lease term of 12 months or less; instead, lease payments are recognised on a straight-line basis. The <strong>low-value asset exemption</strong> allows lessees to elect not to recognise ROU assets and liabilities for leases of individual assets with a fair value below a specified threshold (e.g. personal computers, small office furniture, telephones) when the lessee applies the policy by class of underlying asset.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Lessee ROU Asset and Lease Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Calculating the initial ROU asset and lease liability</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: 5-year equipment lease at $50,000 per year</div><div class="wk-body">A lessee enters a 5-year non-cancellable lease for equipment with annual payments of $50,000 at the end of each year. The implicit rate is not available; the lessee's incremental borrowing rate is 5%. The lessee estimates the asset's fair value at approximately $217,000. The lessee does NOT expect to exercise a purchase option.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Lease liability:</strong> PV of 5 payments of $50,000 at 5% = $50,000 × 4.3295 = <strong>$216,475</strong><br><strong>ROU asset:</strong> $216,475 (lease liability) + $0 (no prepaid payments) + $0 (no initial direct costs) − $0 (no incentives) = <strong>$216,475</strong><br><strong>Finance lease classification:</strong> PV of payments ($216,475) ÷ fair value ($217,000) = <strong>99.8%</strong> — exceeds the 90% threshold. The lease is a FINANCE lease.<br><strong>Income statement impact — Year 1:</strong> Interest expense = $216,475 × 5% = $10,824; amortisation = $216,475 ÷ 5 = $43,295; total expense = <strong>$54,119</strong>. In Year 5, interest is lower and amortisation unchanged, so the total expense front-loads — a key difference from the straight-line expense of an operating lease.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 842 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>ROU assets and lease liabilities, separately for finance and operating leases</span><span>Maturity analysis: future minimum lease payments for each of the next five years and thereafter</span><span>Cost of short-term and low-value leases (when exemptions elected)</span><span>Cash outflows for leases, including interest and principal portions</span><span>Modifications, remeasurements, and transactions not reflected in the lease liability</span><span>For lessors: carrying amount of lease assets by class, and future minimum lease receipts</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a contract contains a lease — determining if an identified asset is implicitly controlled by the lessee</span><span>Whether the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise a purchase, termination, or extension option</span><span>Determining the incremental borrowing rate when the implicit rate is not readily determinable</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</span></div><div class="dot-list"><span>Useful life and residual value of the underlying asset for lessor classification</span><span>Discount rate for lease liability measurement</span><span>Timing and amount of variable lease payments based on an index or rate</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>ASC 842 vs IFRS 16 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 842 vs IFRS 16</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 842)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 16)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Lessee model</span><span>Single model — all leases recognised on balance sheet</span><span>Identical single model — fully converged</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Lessee classification</span><span>Finance vs operating (different expense patterns)</span><span>Single model only; no lessee classification</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Lessor model</span><span>Sales-type / direct financing / operating (three models)</span><span>Single model — all lessors classify as finance leases only</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Sale-leaseback gain recognition</span><span>Gain deferred or recognised depending on lease classification</span><span>Full gain recognised immediately if sale criteria met, regardless of leaseback classification</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Low-value asset exemption</span><span>Available by class of asset</span><span>Available, but less prescriptive thresholds</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Short-term exemption</span><span>Available for leases ≤12 months</span><span>Available for leases ≤12 months</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">ASC 842 and IFRS 16 are the most closely converged standards in the lease accounting space, with full convergence on the lessee side. The lessor model divergence — ASC 842's three-model retention versus IFRS 16's single model — is the single most significant remaining structural difference between the two frameworks for lease accounting.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 842 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Finance lease — ROU asset</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Right-of-use asset depreciated; interest expense recognised</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Operating lease — lessee</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>ROU asset and liability on balance sheet; single lease expense in earnings</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Sale-leaseback — finance lease</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Gain recognised immediately if sale criteria met</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Sale-leaseback — operating lease</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Gain partially or fully deferred</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Lessor — sales-type lease</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Derecognise underlying asset; recognise net investment and profit at commencement</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Lessor — operating lease</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Underlying asset retained; lease revenue straight-line</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 840</span><span>Legacy Leases — superseded standard for historical reference and comparative periods</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 360</span><span>Property, Plant, and Equipment — depreciation of ROU assets and impairment testing</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 470</span><span>Debt — lease liability classification and covenant compliance</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 17</span><span>Leases — superseded IFRS standard replaced by IFRS 16</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 16</span><span>Leases — IFRS counterpart; fully converged on lessee model</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Right-of-use (ROU) asset</div><div class="glossary-def">An asset representing a lessee's right to use an underlying asset for the lease term.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Lease liability</div><div class="glossary-def">The lessee's obligation to make lease payments, measured at the present value of lease payments not yet paid.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Incremental borrowing rate</div><div class="glossary-def">The rate of interest the lessee would have to pay to borrow on a secured basis an amount equal to the lease payments for a similar term and security.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Reasonably certain</div><div class="glossary-def">A higher threshold than 'reasonably possible' but lower than 'virtually certain' — used throughout ASC 842 to assess whether a lessee will exercise an option.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC842 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

